{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787114425,"nonce":"RB5BUCS1nNiI9vwv3EYlSn0QD9I=","title":"**What is wrong.** `channel_threads.state` is documented as `opening | open | frozen | dead`, and `00011:197` says what `frozen` means: *\"frozen means the group closed\"*. Nothing ever writes it. `ThreadRepository.Freeze` exists, is declared on the service port, and has **no production caller** — so a thread stays `open` forever after its group closes, and one of the four states the column admits is unreachable in a running deployment.","message":"**The demonstration.** Grep, not inference. `Freeze` is implemented at `internal/notification/repository/threads.go:323` and declared on the port at `internal/notification/service/ports.go:109`. Every call is a test: `internal/notification/repository/clock_test.go:333` exercises the repository method directly, and `internal/notification/service/digest_tick_test.go:317` is a fake satisfying the interface. The only other `Freeze` in the tree belongs to `internal/drill` and is an unrelated method on a different store. The group-close sweep — the one place that knows a group closed — does not call it.\n\n`freezeThreadSQL` (`threads.go:316-319`) is therefore dead SQL, and the `frozen` arm of `threads_dead_ck`'s sibling state vocabulary is dead schema.\n\n**Why it matters.** The state exists to answer \"may oto still write here?\". A closed generation's thread should stop accepting replies, and `frozen` is how the schema says so; with nothing setting it, the only thing standing between a closed group and a new reply is that nothing currently tries. That is a property held by accident rather than by the column that was added to hold it. It also makes the column lie to anyone reading the schema to understand thread lifecycle — the same class of defect as `00057`'s header promising live flap columns, and it is the reason `git-bug e05aaad` was filed.\n\nThere is a second cost. `ThreadStore.Freeze` on the port is an interface method every implementation must satisfy — including fakes — for a call that never happens, so it is paid for at every seam and buys nothing.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/repository/threads.go:316-319` (`freezeThreadSQL`), `:323` (`Freeze`) · `internal/notification/service/ports.go:109` (the port declaration) · `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:197` (the column comment that promises it) · `internal/notification/repository/clock_test.go:333` and `internal/notification/service/digest_tick_test.go:317` (the only callers, both tests) · the group-close path in `internal/grouping` (the caller that does not exist).\n\n⚠️ Found while mapping the conversation-identity surface for `7570090` stage 3, not by running anything. Whether a reply to a closed generation's thread is actually reachable today was NOT determined — that is the first thing to establish.\n\n**Scope.** small to medium — the code is one call, but the decision is whether `frozen` is a state oto wants at all now that `7570090` proposes to delete `alert_groups` and re-key conversations. Freezing on group-close may be the wrong rule under a policy-collapsed conversation that outlives any one generation.\n\n**Done when.** Either the group-close path freezes its threads and a test asserts a frozen thread refuses a further reply, or `frozen` is removed from the state vocabulary along with `Freeze`, `freezeThreadSQL` and the port method, and `00011:197`'s comment is corrected by a new migration. What must not persist is a documented state that nothing can enter. If `7570090` lands first, the decision is taken in its terms: a conversation keyed by a policy collapse does not close when one generation does, so \"frozen\" would need a new trigger or none.","files":null}]}